# Era 10 — Observability & Operations (V32–V35)

_Phase 2 · Concept Layer V1_

**Status: ✅ done, verified 2026-09-17**

**Implementation note:** all four pages written new — `concepts/observability.mdx` (V32,
the build/application/machine log taxonomy front and center per the topic's own emphasis),
`concepts/state-and-drift.mdx` (V33, two-pass disclosure, written directly in
`DISCLOSURE.md`'s own compliant register — "shows what has changed... one row per
difference"), `concepts/rollback-promotion-and-recovery.mdx` (V34, paired with
`guides/rollback-and-recovery.mdx`, the release-vs-configuration rollback distinction as its
opening frame), `concepts/automation.mdx` (V35, links to `cli/shared-flags.mdx` rather than
restating flag semantics, per Invariant 1's altitude rule).

Covers T27–T29, T32: Observability, State and drift, Rollback/promotion/recovery,
Automation. Grouped together because all four are cross-cutting operational concerns rather
than a single feature area, and T28 (State and drift) is flagged by the sibling roadmap as
one of its own highest-disclosure-risk families (`statecmd`, `projectcontract`) — this era
inherits that same caution one layer up.

---

## V32 — Observability (T27)

### Goal
New concept page: build vs. application vs. machine logs, metrics and units, traffic
reports, live watching, filters, time ranges, interpreting missing or stale data.

### Reuses
`cli/logs.mdx`/`cli/metrics.mdx`/`cli/traffic.mdx`/`cli/observe.mdx`/`cli/history.mdx`,
pending the sibling roadmap's own resolution of these as `observability` subcommands vs.
standalone pages (its Era 1 naming-ambiguity note) — link to whichever structure survives.

### Implement
`concepts/observability.mdx`: the three log types as a clear taxonomy (build/application/
machine) since users conflating them is a common confusion point named directly in the
topic, "interpreting missing or stale data" as its own subsection (a genuinely useful,
fully disclosure-safe topic — this is exactly the kind of "what you'll see and what it
means" content the whole roadmap exists to provide).

### Validate
Terminology matches whatever the sibling roadmap's Era 8 (`observability` family) settles on
by write time — checked, not assumed, since that era may not have landed first.

### Done when
Page exists; log-type taxonomy is unambiguous and matches reference-layer terminology.

---

## V33 — State and drift (T28)

### Goal
New concept page: local/saved/running state as three named states, detecting differences,
what pull/apply/reconcile/rollback change, conflicts and safe recovery.

### Reuses
`cli/status.mdx`, `statecmd` family, `projectcontract` family — the sibling roadmap's own
highest-disclosure-risk era (its Era 9). This version does not re-derive that era's
disclosure work; it applies the same discipline one layer up, using the compliant/
non-compliant worked example already given in `/features/docs/DISCLOSURE.md` ("drift shows
what has changed... one row per difference" vs. "diffing the reconciler's desired-state
graph...") as the literal register to write this page in.

### Implement
`concepts/state-and-drift.mdx`: local/saved/running as three plainly-named states, "drift"
explained purely as the observable difference between what was last applied and what's
running now (the compliant example's exact framing), "what pull/apply/reconcile/rollback
change" stated as before/after outcomes for each command, "conflicts and safe recovery" as
concrete user-facing steps. Checkpoint terms kept fully out: `reconcile` (as a *mechanism*
word — the *command name* `reconcile` can still be named since it's public CLI surface, but
never explained by describing its internal process), `drift` (same — nameable as a term,
never explained mechanically), `desired state`, `contract`.

### Validate
Two-read disclosure pass — mandatory, not optional, given this is the sibling roadmap's own
flagged highest-risk vocabulary reused here.

### Done when
Page exists; passes two-read disclosure; every sentence checked individually against the
`DISCLOSURE.md` worked example's register.

---

## V34 — Rollback, promotion, and recovery (T29)

### Goal
New concept page: which actions restore application releases vs. configuration, moving
between environments, recovering failed operations, whether application data is affected.

### Reuses
`cli/recover.mdx`, `guides/rollback-and-recovery.mdx` (existing task guide), `statecmd
rollback` / `projectcontract rollback` (per V33's disclosure register, reused here).

### Implement
`concepts/rollback-promotion-and-recovery.mdx`: the release-vs-configuration rollback
distinction as the opening framing (a common point of confusion — "did that roll back my
code or my settings?"), promotion explained as moving a build between environments (an
observable action), "whether application data is affected" stated explicitly for each
action type — this is a direct data-safety question and must not be hedged or omitted.
Bidirectional link with `guides/rollback-and-recovery.mdx` confirmed at Era 12.

### Validate
Disclosure pass consistent with V33's register (this page sits directly downstream of it,
vocabulary-wise). Data-affected claims verified per Invariant 1 given the stakes (same
seriousness as V29's destructive-action list).

### Done when
Page exists; data-affected claims verified; bidirectional link confirmed at Era 12.

---

## V35 — Automation (T32)

### Goal
New concept page: commands that work without prompts, required flags, output formats, exit
codes, wait/timeout behavior, confirmations, examples suitable for scripts or CI.

### Reuses
Shared-flags reference from the sibling roadmap's Era 2 (V8 there — `--wait`, `--yes`,
`--json`, `--quiet`, etc.) as the direct link target; this page is the concept-level "why and
when to script Beaver" framing above that flag-by-flag reference.

### Implement
`concepts/automation.mdx`: frames scriptability as a first-class use case (CI/CD, cron,
infra-as-code style workflows), exit-code conventions and output-format choices explained as
what a script author needs to branch on, links to the shared-flags reference rather than
re-listing every flag (Invariant 1 of this roadmap — concept altitude, not reference
altitude).

### Validate
Confirms no flag behavior is restated in a way that could drift from the shared-flags
reference's own wording — this page links, it does not paraphrase authoritative flag
semantics.

### Done when
Page exists; closes Era 10.
